## 3. THERMAL ISOLATION, REFRACTORY, & CHASSIS ENVELOPE
To safely maintain an internal processing core temperature of 1,600°F to 2,000°F (870°C to 1,100°C) within a Laminated Strand Lumber (LSL) structural chassis, the destructor utilizes a layered refractory matrix and a dynamic fluidic air barrier.
## 3.1 Layered Refractory Stackup

* Alumina Ceramic Firebrick: The primary pyrolysis retort liner is built from dense, high-purity alumina ceramic firebrick to handle chemical stress and the mechanical scouring of medical waste inputs.
* Hydrophobic Silica Aerogel Insulation: Wrapped directly around the ceramic core is a 2.0-inch thick layer of high-temperature silica aerogel blanketing. Its low thermal conductivity (~0.015 W/mK) significantly limits conductive heat bleed into the room.
* Infrared Radiant Mirror: The outermost surface of the insulated retort assembly is wrapped in a highly polished 321 Stainless Steel foil shield, which reflects infrared energy back inward toward the core.

## 3.2 Two-Stage Fluidic Counter-Current Air Barrier
A 4-inch open air gap separates the insulated reactor housing from the structural walls, governed by both passive and active safety loops:

* Passive Convective Draft: A shielded, downward-facing air intake manifold in the floor plate draws cool outdoor air into the base of the open air gap. As the air absorbs minimal radiant heat from the foil mirror, it expands and rises through the stack effect toward high-wall ceiling exhaust vents.
* Active Emergency Air Curtains: Low-voltage 12V variable-speed blowers are integrated directly into the chassis floor rails. If analog bimetallic snap-sensors detect the outer air-gap channel crossing a safety threshold of 110°F (43°C), the active fans engage, injecting a planar cold air stream up the inner face of the LSL timber studs to isolate the structural walls.

