## 5. PRE-INCINERATION LOW-TEMPERATURE DEHYDRATION STAGE
Medical waste often contains high moisture levels (up to 60%–80% liquid weight), which can cause severe temperature drops if introduced directly into a pyrolysis chamber. To solve this thermodynamic bottleneck, the destructor utilizes a dual-stage pre-treatment dehydration hopper.

 [MOIST BIOHAZARD WASTE] ──► [DEHYDRATION HOPPER (220°F)] ──► [PURE CLEAN WATER COIL]
                                         ▲                                │
                                         │ (600°F Exhaust Jacket)         ▼
                        [SYNGAS TURBINE RECOVERY ENGINE] ──► [COMMUNAL WATER STORAGE]

## 5.1 Low-Grade Evaporation Loop
Prior to entering the primary retort, waste is loaded into a pre-treatment hopper wrapped in a stainless steel hydronic jacket. This jacket routes the 600°F–800°F clean thermal exhaust exiting the syngas feedback loop, gently baking the waste between 220°F and 250°F (104°C to 121°C). This process boils away all internal moisture without catching fire or melting plastics.
## 5.2 Moisture Recovery Distiller
The clean water vapor driven off the drying waste is drawn via vacuum into a secondary condensing coil. Because this water is evaporated at high temperatures, it acts as an auxiliary distiller, outputting sterile, clean water that pipes directly into the community's drinking water storage barrels. Once dried, a mechanical slide-gate drops the bone-dry, pre-warmed medical biomass into the core, where it cracks instantly into rich syngas.
