## 6. RECOVERY GENERATION & SYNGAS RECOVERY MATRIX## 6.1 Closed-Loop Syngas Feedback Propulsion
During pyrolysis, the dry biomedical textiles and plastics break down into a combustible synthesis gas (syngas). The gas is vacuum-drawn through a high-velocity ceramic cyclonic scrubber to trap carbon fly-ash and tars, routing the clean gas directly back into an array of burners beneath the retort.
This enables a self-sustaining cycle: solar energy initializes the reactor to 1,600°F during the day, and the energy density of the waste itself takes over via syngas propulsion to maintain 1,800°F deep into the night or through heavy winter storms.
## 6.2 Community Thermal Export
The exhaust gas exiting the clean syngas loop passes through a counter-flow heat exchanger wrapping the destructor's exhaust flue, capturing clean 600°F fluid. This thermal energy is piped across the community grid through an insulated sleeve directly into the Bathing Pod and Solar Distillery V1.0, outputting enough thermal overplus to distill hundreds of gallons of fresh water without drawing from the camp's LiFePO4 batteries.
